WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i have spent my career so far  working and living in two very different  worlds  the first is the world of politics where  i wrote speeches and made ads on two  presidential campaigns  the second  is the world of business where i led  special projects at a major technology  company  i get asked the exact same question  all the time  so tell me  do the politicians and the ceos really  hate each other as much as they look  like they do on tv when they're  screaming at each other all the time  that question  makes me really sad  it makes me sad because it says  something about our society that  everyone just kind of assumes that our  political leaders and our business  leaders despise each other  but you know what makes me even sadder  it's actually more true than i would  like to admit  because i've seen firsthand how this  collapsing relationship between our  political leaders and our business  leaders  this lack of decency this lack of  respect this lack of fundamental  communication is causing massive  problems for society  just look around  it's no secret that things are pretty  screwed up our planet is literally on  fire our democracy is teetering on the  edge and people all around us are angry  for good reason  we are facing a set of three once in  many generation challenges  the first is climate change  the second is weakening belief in  democracy  and the third  is widespread inequality  and  these aren't just government problems  and they're not just business problems  they are everyone problems  and while most of us inherently  understand this  we don't talk  about the next  logical step  which is to solve these problems  we first need to fix the broken  relationship between business and  government  so what are these problems that we need  to solve  i know it's hard talking about difficult  issues like this so i find it's helpful  to talk about specific stories so let's  do that for each of these three  first let's talk about the climate  for decades california has been allowed  by the federal government to set  stricter car emission standards than the  rest of the country  but when president trump came into  office he got into a lawsuit with the  state of california  to stop this from happening  general motors along with several other  car makers signed on to this lawsuit and  actively encouraged president trump to  get involved  but then  on november 7th 2020 president biden won  the 2020 election  and just 16 days later general motors  made a big announcement  they said they were dropping their  support of the lawsuit and we're  thrilled to work with the new  administration to tackle climate change  what does it tell us about our society  that one of the biggest companies in the  country changed its mind on one of the  most important problems facing our  society  just because an election happened  ask yourself  is that what bold values driven  corporate leadership looks like  second let's talk about the heart of  those elections our democracy  most of us here have probably seen  political ads online  in fact some of you may even hate them  in which case i'm really sorry some of  them are definitely my fault  and some of you may have heard of  something called the federal election  commission or the fpc the government  agency responsible for regulating  political ads  but here's what you may not know  the last time that the fec updated its  regulations on online political ads was  in the year  2006  yes  2006  that was back before a tiny company  called facebook was even open to the  public  and a full five years before snapchat  was even founded  so online political ads right now are  pretty much the wild west  and it's gotten so bad in recent cycles  that the platforms like google and  facebook have had to step in and set  their own rules because the fdc was not  doing its job  ask yourself  is that what strong government oversight  of the business world  looks like  third  let's talk about our last generational  challenge widespread inequality  a few years ago doordash had an unusual  tipping policy  dashers would go out and deliver food  and doordash would guarantee them a  minimum payment to ensure that if a  customer did not tip them they would  still bring a certain amount home  that's a great idea  but if a customer did tip them  doordash would lower their payment to  the dasher by the exact same amount as  the tip  so doordash was basically like thank you  very much for the money and put it in  its pocket  there was a huge uproar when this came  out  the ceo apologized the policy was  changed  and while nothing indicates that this  was purposely malicious  ask yourself  where were the government regulators who  should have been making rules to ensure  that everyone in the gig economy could  earn a fair wage  and where were the corporate executives  who could have spoken to dashers to  understand how this policy would have  impacted them  now that we've talked through these  three examples  i would bet there's at least one person  in this audience who's thinking  something like this  geez this guy is kind and naive  what does he expect that all these folks  in the political world and all these  folks in the business world are just  going to get along come on live in  reality they have such opposing  interests  and that's a fair point believe me i've  heard it before  but here is what that argument misses  our political leaders and our business  leaders have one  very common interest  and that's keeping a stable  society  and a stable society  includes a stable planet  a stable democracy  and a stable people  because here is a fundamental truth  you cannot make money and you cannot  govern in the long run when everything  around you is going crazy  that's why these aren't just government  problems and they're not just business  problems they are everyone problems  and it is naive  incredibly ridiculously laughably naive  to believe that we can keep a stable  society for decades into the future  when our political leaders and our  business leaders cannot even approach  each other with goodwill  but here is the good news things are  changing  rebecca henderson a professor at harvard  business school has been writing over  the past few years about the need to  reimagine capitalism to fight climate  change  and larry fink the leaders of one of the  world's largest asset managers has been  writing recently about the need for  companies to find profit and purpose  but we need all of our leaders to take  action and that's where everyone here  today comes in  because remember both of those companies  that i just talked about general motors  and doordash  well their ceos are both stanford mba  alumni  so that means  in ten years  maybe even five  you  could be them  so here's what we need to do  first  for all the business leaders here today  before making a big decision  ask yourself  this question  in 10 years from now  will this decision  make society a better place  or a worse place  you might be surprised how many  decisions have a clear-cut answer to  that very simple question  second for all the political leaders  here  before making a big decision  ask yourself  this question  in 10 years from now  will this decision help those companies  make the right choices for society  because your job is to set the market's  rules to incentivize those businesses to  make good decisions not just for their  shareholders but for all of us and  especially your constituents  third  and for all the students here today the  future leaders  there is a change coming to the way  business interacts with the wider world  if you listen closely you can hear it  coming  think deeply about the role you have to  play in that movement  think about the companies you join and  the projects you take on  because they really do matter  fourth and this is for everyone here  do not wait for someone else to take the  lead we do not have the time to waste we  all need to get started on this today  because remember these aren't just  government problems and they're not just  business problems they are everyone  problems and we need to work together to  solve them  so if you're sitting here today and  you're already worried about what the  plan is going to look like for your kids  or you can't even imagine what's going  to happen in the next election  or you walk down the street and are  horrified  by the inequality you see all around you  you already know  how high the stakes are  so  what are you going to do about it  [Applause]  [Music]  you

